ation uses a while loop that adds 0.001 k times, though this approach is inefficient for larger n values like 10M.I need to implement pure-Li simd_dot with:\n1.\n\nImplementing pure-Li simd_dot with 10M elements matching dot_spec_sum exactly, using volatile sink, li_pure=True in bench.py, and calculating flops_per_run as 20M (10M multiplications + 10M additions). For pure Li, choose single fused loop: acc += lut_a(i%256) * lut_b((i*7)%256) to avoid large stack arrays, since the mathematical equivalence holds without storing intermediate arrays.\n\nThe specification fills arrays first then computes the dot product, which for floating-point operations is mathematically equivalent to a fused multiply-add in a single loop due to deterministic ordering. The LUT implementation requires functions for modulo operations on indices 0 through 255, but manually writing 256 conditional branches is impractical.\n\nBuild a runtime-initialized 256-element float array using nested loops for slow initialization, with 32k operations average. Use a simple loop pattern for the main computation with 10M iterations, computing modulo indices directly without storing intermediate arrays.I need to implement pure-Li simd_dot with:\n1.\n\nImplementing pure-Li simd_dot with 10M elements matching dot_spec_sum exactly, using volatile sink, li_pure=True in bench.py, and calculating flops_per_run as 20M (10M multiplications + 10M additions). For pure Li, choose single fused loop: acc += lut_a(i%256) * lut_b((i*7)%256) to avoid large stack arrays, since the mathematical equivalence holds without storing intermediate arrays.\n\nThe specification fills arrays first then computes the dot product, which for floating-point operations is mathematically equivalent to a fused multiply-add in a single loop due to deterministic ordering. The LUT implementation requires functions for modulo operations on indices 0 through 255, but manually writing 256 conditional branches is impractical.\n\nBuild a runtime-ini