them could help. From the matmul pattern, they use 256x256 arrays with variable indices A[i][j], which involve significant memory usage.\n\nCalling `li_rt_volatile_sink_f64` in the loop every N iterations helps prevent dead code elimination, but only if it actually affects the accumulator within the loop. Otherwise, the compiler might still optimize away the loop entirely.\n\nTo ensure the accumulator remains live, the volatile sink must have a side effect inside the loop. Options include periodically sinking the accumulator's value or using an external C function for part of the multiplication.I need to fix DCE by using memory arrays similar to matmul, storing products in a small ring buffer or using an array[256] for partial sums.\n\nUse a 256-element array to accumulate partial sums, summing at the end to avoid memory traffic issues. Alternatively, replicate the dot_core.c approach by allocating and filling arrays, though 160MB may exceed Li's heap support. Use tiled arrays with correct initialization: for each 256-element tile starting at base = chunk*256, fill arrays a and b with appropriate values and compute dot products.\n\nSince base is always a multiple of 256, (base+off) & 255 simplifies to off, making the calculation straightforward. Similarly, b[off] = (off*7)%256 * 0.002 equals dot_lut_b7(off), which remains constant foldable.\n\nTo address memory dependencies that LLVM cannot analyze, use array storage with 256-element tiles for both a and b. Initialize these arrays within a loop over chunks, filling each tile with corresponding values from dot_lut_a and dot_lut_b7 before computing the dot product. This approach helps manage memory traffic and enables efficient computation.\n\nThe loop `off = 0; while off < 256: acc = acc + tile_a[off] * tile_b[off]; off = off + 1` may still fold if `lut` is pure.\n\nIf `tile_a[off] = dot_lut_a((chunk + off) % 256)` it would be incorrect according to the spec.\n\nUsing `tile_a[off] = dot_lut_a(off) + 0.0 * float(chunk)` could prevent folding if `chunk` is not constant across iterations.\n\nAlternatively, implement a noinline C helper function or read from global mutable state to avoid folding.\n\nThe key idea is to prevent LLVM from optimizing away the tile computation by ensuring variables remain non-constant or introduce external dependencies.\n\nThe simplest industrial fix is to use `extern proc li_rt_volatile_sink_f64` called every iteration to prevent optimization. Although expensive with 10M volatile writes, it ensures correctness and works for verification purposes. The volatile write forces computation of `acc` each time, even if printing only occurs when environment variables are set.I need to fix DCE by using memory arrays similar to matmul, storing products in a small ring buffer or using an array[256] for partial sums.\n\nUse a 256-element array to accumulate partial sums, summing at the end to avoid memory traffic issues. Alternatively, replicate the dot_core.c approach by allocating and filling arrays, though 160MB may exceed Li's heap support. Use tiled arrays with correct initialization: for each 256-element tile starting at base = chunk*256, fill arrays a and b with appropriate values and compute dot products.\n\nSince base is always a multiple of 256, (base+off) & 255 simplifies to off, making the calculation straightforward. Similarly, b[off] = (off*7)%256 * 0.002 equals dot_lut_b7(off), which remains constant foldable.\n\nTo address memory dependencies that LLVM cannot analyze, use array storage with 256-element tiles for both a and b. Initialize these arrays within a loop over chunks, filling each tile with corresponding values from dot_lut_a and dot_lut_b7 before computing the dot product. This approach helps manage memory traffic and enables efficient computation.\n\nThe loop `off = 0; while off < 256: acc = acc + tile_a[off] * tile_b[off]; off = off + 1` may still fold if `lut` is pure.\n\nIf `tile_a[off] = dot_lut_a((chunk + off) % 256)` it would be incorrect according to the spec.\n\nUsing `tile_a[off] = dot_lut_a(off) + 0.0 * float(chunk)` could prevent folding if `chunk` is not constant across iterations.\n\nAlternatively, implement a noinline C helper function or read from global mutable state to avoid folding.\n\nThe key idea is to prevent LLVM from optimizing away the tile computation by ensuring variables remain non-constant or introduce external dependencies.\n\nThe simplest industrial fix is to use `extern proc li_rt_volatile_sink_f64` called every iteration to prevent optimization. Although expensive with 10M volatile writes, it ensures correctness and works for verification purposes. The volatile write forces computation of `acc` each time, even if printing only occurs when environment variables are set.The file contains 39,062 lines of output.The file contains 39,062 lines of output.Still 0 and 0.0024 sec - still DCE'd even with array tiles and per-chunk volatile sink!Still 0 and 0.0024 sec - still DCE'd even with array tiles and per-chunk volatile sink!Matmul runs in 0.002 seconds and produces the correct checksum of 161055.\n\nThe issue isn't solely about wall timeour simd_dot produces an incorrect result of 0. Matmul uses array writes A[i][j_init] with varying i and j indices over a 256256 memory layout, while our simd_dot may still constant-fold LUT lookups.\n\nAttempting to disable fast-math for simd in a test build or using the exact dot_core.c pattern with pure li and heap allocation (which isn't available) could help.\n\nTo align with the specification where global i = chunk*256 + off:\n- lut_a should use off only (since base % 256 == 0)\n- lut_b should use (off * 7) % 256 only\n\nThe mathematical result per chunk is consistent across all chunks since each computes the same 256-element dot product with identical values of f(i%256) and g((i*7)%256). As a result, LLVM optimizes by multiplying the sum of f(off)*g(off*7) by 39062, which introduces a bug when the computation is incorrect.\n\nThe fix 