        "message": {
          "text": "Matmul runs in 0.002 seconds and produces the correct checksum of 161055.\n\nThe issue isn't solely about wall timeour simd_dot produces an incorrect result of 0. Matmul uses array writes A[i][j_init] with varying i and j indices over a 256256 memory layout, while our simd_dot may still constant-fold LUT lookups.\n\nAttempting to disable fast-math for simd in a test build or using the exact dot_core.c pattern with pure li and heap allocation (which isn't available) could help.\n\nTo align with the specification where global i = chunk*256 + off:\n- lut_a should use off only (since base % 256 == 0)\n- lut_b should use (off * 7) % 256 only\n\nThe mathematical result per chunk is consistent across all chunks since each computes the same 256-element dot product with identical values of f(i%256) and g((i*7)%256). As a result, LLVM optimizes by multiplying the sum of f(off)*g(off*7) by 39062, which introduces a bug when the computation is incorrect.\n\nThe fix involves replacing the repeated chunk pattern with a single loop that iterates through varying indices instead of repeating the same calculation multiple times. This ensures accurate computation without relying on constant folding of repeated patterns.",
