          "text": "Interesting - PERF.md indicates that Li links md_core.c through LI_EXTRA_C, whereas bench.py designates md_lennard_jones with `li_pure=True`.\n\nThere's a conflict between PERF.md and bench.py regarding Li's linking approach. The verify task requires running tier-2 verification for md_lennard_jones and heat_equation_2d, but currently fails due to Li drivers not passing verification.\n\nTo resolve this:\n1. Extend verify.py to handle the specified test case\n2. Fix Li drivers to pass verification\n\nFor md_lennard_jones with li_pure=True, either:\n- Implement a pure Li MD version matching native C checksums, or\n- Change li_pure to False to use C kernel as indicated in PERF.md\n\nSince PERF.md shows Li using LI_EXTRA_C, changing li_pure=False in bench.py should align with existing setup.\n\nmain.li should call li_md_kernel and sink the checksum using li_rt_volatile_sink_f64.\n\nHowever, if li_pure=False, the li binary will attempt --verify before execution, but the Li main doesn't parse argv for --verify. Therefore, either add --verify support via argc in Li (if available) or use li_rt_volatile_sink_f64 with li_md_checksum() extern.\n\nFor shared C kernel benches, the pattern is:\n- Declare extern procs for the kernel and checksum\n- Call the kernel in main\n- Sink the checksum using li_rt_volatile_sink_f64\n\nFor `md` with `li_pure=False`, the Li main calls the C kernel and sinks the checksum using `li_rt_volatile_sink_f64`. However, since `md` is marked `li_pure=True`, it should be a pure Li implementation without the C kernel, aligning with the long-term goal of pure Li outperforming C++. \n\nA pragmatic fix for `wave-b-tier2-verify` involves changing `md` to `li_pure=False` to use the C kernel, fixing `heat_equation_2d`'s `main.li`, and extending `verify.py` to support tier-2 smoke tests.",
